# Manuscript PDF page 121

                          LEARNING TO HOLD



    The DJ nodded at him. The kid grinned. He went to stand at the
edge of the room, as people do who contributed the second copy and
won’t be in the history.
    Then the tall teenager started the record.
    It was a drum break. A few seconds of nothing but percussion, bon‐
gos and congas and a driving guitar, the part of a song most people
don’t get long enough to love. He let it play. And just before it ended,
when the singer would have come back in, he caught the record on the
other turntable and dropped it at the start of the break, again, and
again. He didn’t play the song. He held the best part of it. He kept
dropping the needle on the best eight seconds of the record and held
that room in the air for as long as it had breath.
    I watched seventy kids go off like a flare.
    And I heard it. The length. About the same as the loop in the bo‐
dega. About the same as the one under my block. A repeating thing, a
cycle of eight seconds, and nobody in that room was pressed flat by it.
They were up on their feet.
    Somebody had found the same length from the other direction.
    “A loop isn’t the enemy,” Rafa said quietly, beside me, on the side‐
walk, in the present, with the Cross Bronx grumbling behind him. “Say
it back to me.”
    I said it back to him, slower than he had.
    “What matters is what’s inside it.” He took his hand off the wall. His
palm had a faint gray of brick on it. “That’s the whole thing I know.
Everything else is footnotes.”
    I stood with my own hand against the brick. I didn’t take it away yet.
    Across the street, at the corner, under a streetlight, a man in a ball
cap stood with his hands in his pockets, watching us.
    I knew the shoulders before I knew the face. He wasn’t pretending
not to be looking. He stood there in the orange light, calm, a screen
between channels, and for a long moment neither of us did anything.
    Then I lifted two fingers to my left temple.
    He went very still.
    Then, slowly, he lifted his own.
    Dos. I’m here.



